<section xml:id="sec-signed-borel">
<title>Signed Borel measures on <m>\mathbb{R}</m> and functions of bounded variation</title>
<p>In <xref ref="sec-borel-on-R"/> we studied the Borel measures on closed intervals in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>. In particular, we showed that if <m>\mu</m> is a Borel measure on closed interval <m>[a,b]</m> then the cumulative distribution function for <m>\mu</m> is non-negative, increasing, and right-continuous, <xref ref="thm-cdf" /> Conversely, if <m>g</m> is a non-negative, increasing, right-continuous function on <m>[a,b]</m> then <m>g</m> uniquely determines a Borel measure on <m>[a,b]</m> whose cumulative distribution function is <m>g</m>, <xref ref="cor-cdf-and-Borel" />. In this section we want to study the relationship between <em>signed</em> Borel measures on closed intervals and functions on <m>[a,b]</m>.</p>
<p>We begin with a definition to formalize our language.</p>

<definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>X</m> be a topological space and let <m>\mathcal{B}(X)</m> be the <m>\sigma</m>-algebra of Borel sets of <m>X</m>.
            A signed measure <m>\nu</m> on <m>(X,\mathcal{B}(X))</m> is a <term>signed Borel measure</term> if <m>|\nu(K)|&lt;\infty</m> for all compact sets <m>K \subseteq X</m>.
            <idx><h>signed measure</h><h>signed Borel measure</h></idx>
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>

<remark>
    <p>
        If <m>\nu</m> is a signed Borel measure on <m>(X,\mathcal{B}(X))</m> and <m>X</m> is compact, then <m>|\nu(X)|&lt; \infty</m>.
        It follows that <m>|\nu|(X) &lt; \infty</m>.
        In particular, if <m>\nu</m> is a signed Borel measure on the closed interval <m>[a,b]</m> then <m>\nu</m> is of bounded variation.
    </p>
</remark>

<p>When studying Borel measures on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, the functions that arose were increasing. For signed measures, the functions that we will need to study are functions of bounded variation. We define the necessary terms now.</p>

<definition>
    <notation>
        <usage><m>V(f,P)</m></usage>
      <description>The variation of a function <m>f</m> over a partition <m>P</m></description>
    </notation>

    <notation>
        <usage><m>TV(f)</m></usage>
      <description>The total variation of a function <m>f</m></description>
    </notation>

    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>f \colon [a,b] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> be a function.
            If <m>P = \{a= x_0 &lt; \ldots &lt; x_n =b\}</m> is a partition of <m>[a,b]</m> the <term>variation of <m>f</m> over <m>P</m></term><idx><h>variation</h></idx> is
                <md> V(f,P) = \sum_{k=1}^n |f(x_i) - f(x_{i-1})|.</md>
            The <term>total variation of <m>f</m></term> is defined as
            <md> TV(f) = \sup \{ V(f,P) \colon P \text{ a partition of }[a,b]\}.</md>
            When <m>TV(f)&lt;\infty</m> then <m>f</m> is of <term>bounded variation</term>.
            <idx><h>total variation</h><h>of a function</h></idx>
            <idx><h>bounded variation</h><h>for a function</h></idx>
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>

<p>The next example, whilst simple, is very important.</p>

<example xml:id="ex-inc-has-bounded-variation">
<p>If <m>f \colon [a,b] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> is monotone (i.e. <m>f</m> is increasing or decreasing), then <m>f</m> is of bounded variation.
    Indeed, if <m>f</m> is increasing and <m>P = \{a= x_0 &lt; \ldots &lt; x_n = b\}</m> is any partition of <m>[a,b]</m> then
<md> V(f,P) = \sum_{k=1}^\infty |f(x_i) -f(x_{i-1})| = f(b) - f(a).</md>
Thus, <m>TV(f) = f(b)-f(a)</m>.
    If <m>f</m> is decreasing then we can show that
<md>TV(f) = f(a) - f(b).</md>
</p>
</example>

<p>Intuitively, the total variation of a function <m>f</m> can be seen as a measure of the oscillation of <m>f</m>: the more oscillation the higher the value of <m>TV(f)</m>. Monotone functions, as in <xref ref="ex-inc-has-bounded-variation"/> do not oscillate and so have bounded variation. To find examples of functions which are not of bounded variation we look at functions with a lot of oscillation.</p>
<example xml:id="ex-not-tv-1">
<p>Define <m>f</m> on <m>[0,1]</m> by
<md> f(x) = \begin{cases} \sin(\frac{1}{x}) &amp; x \neq 0 \\
    0 &amp; x = 0.\end{cases}</md>
Then <m>f</m> is not of bounded variation. See <xref ref="exer-not-tv-1" />.</p>
</example>
<example xml:id="ex-not-tv-2">
<p>Define <m>g</m> on <m>[0,1]</m> by
<md> g(x) = \begin{cases} x\sin(\frac{1}{x}) &amp; x \neq 0 \\
    0 &amp; x = 0.\end{cases}</md>
Then <m>g</m> is not of bounded variation. See <xref ref="exer-not-tv-2" />.</p>
</example>

<p>Note that <xref ref="ex-not-tv-2" /> shows that there are continuous functions which are not of bounded variation, see <xref ref="exer-not-tv-2" />. However the continuous functions which are of bounded variation are bountiful, as the next proposition shows.</p>

<proposition xml:id="prop-bv-vec-sp">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>[a,b]</m> be a closed interval in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
            If <m>f,g \colon [a,b] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> are functions of bounded variation then
                <md> \alpha f + \beta g </md>
            is of bounded variation for all <m>\alpha, \beta \in \mathbb{R}</m>.        
        </p>
    </statement>

    <proof>
<p>Let <m>P = \{a=x_0 &lt; \ldots &lt; x_n =b\}</m> be a partition of <m>[a,b]</m>.
    Then
<md>
\begin{aligned}
V(f+g, P) &amp;= \sum_{k=1}^n |f(x_i) + g(x_i) - f(x_{i-1}) - g(x_{i-1})| \\
        &amp;\leq \sum_{k=1}^n|f(x_i) - f(x_{i-1})| + \sum_{k=1}^n|g(x_i) - g(x_{i-1})|.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Thus
<md> TV(f+g) \leq TV(f) + TV(g) &lt; \infty.</md>
Thus <m>f+g</m> is of bounded variation.</p>
<p>Take any scalar <m>\alpha \in \mathbb{R}</m>.
    Then
<md>
\begin{aligned}
V(\alpha f,P) = \sum_{k=1}^n|(\alpha f)(x_i) - (\alpha f)(x_{i-1})| &amp; = |\alpha|V(f,P)|.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Thus <m>TV(\alpha f, P) = |\alpha|TV(f,P) &lt; \infty</m>.
    The result follows.</p>
</proof>
</proposition>

<p>Knowing that increasing functions have bounded variation (<xref ref="ex-inc-has-bounded-variation" />) and that the functions of bounded variation form a vector space (<xref ref="prop-bv-vec-sp" />), gives many functions which are of bounded variation. Examples include the sine function (<xref ref="exer-sin-bv" />), the cosine function (<xref ref="exer-cos-bv" />), and polynomials (<xref ref="exer-poly-bv" />).</p>

<p>The following lemma gives a partial relationship between Borel signed measures and functions of bounded variation.</p>

<lemma xml:id="lem-signed-meas-to-bv">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>\nu</m> be a Borel signed measure on the closed interval <m>[a,b]</m>.
            Define a function
                <md> f(x) = \nu([a,x]). </md>
            Then <m>f</m> is the difference of two increasing functions right-continuous.
            In particular, <m>f</m> is right-continuous and of bounded variation.        
        </p>
    </statement>

    <proof>
<p>Let <m>\nu = \nu_+ - \nu_-</m> be the Jordan decomposition of <m>\nu</m>.
    Let <m>f_1</m> be the cumulative distribution of <m>\nu_+</m>, and let <m>f_2</m> be the cumulative distribution function for <m>\nu_-</m>.
    Then
<md>
\begin{aligned}
f(x) &amp;= \nu([a,x]) \\
        &amp;= \nu_+([a,x]) - \nu_-([a,x])\\
        &amp;= f_1(x) - f_2(x).
\end{aligned}
</md>
By <xref ref="thm-cdf" />, <m>f_1</m> and <m>f_2</m> are increasing, right-continuous functions.
    By <xref ref="ex-inc-has-bounded-variation" /> <m>f_1</m> and <m>f_2</m> are of bounded variation, and so <m>f</m> is of bounded variation by <xref ref="prop-bv-vec-sp" />.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>

<remark>
<p>An alternate way to prove <m>f(x) = \nu([a,x])</m> is of bounded variation in <xref ref="lem-signed-meas-to-bv" /> would be to invoke <xref ref="prop-bv-signed-meas-as-sup" /> directly.</p>
</remark>

<p>To complete the picture of the relationship between functions of bounded variation and Borel measures of bounded variation, we need another description of functions of functions of bounded variation.</p>
<p>Let <m>f</m> be a function of bounded variation on <m>[a,b]</m>. Then, for each <m>x \in [a,b]</m>, the restriction of <m>f</m> to <m>[a,x]</m> is also of bounded variation. Define the function <m>T_f</m> on <m>[a,b]</m> by
<md> T_f(x) = TV(f|_{[a,x]})</md>
We call <m>T_f</m> the <term>function of total variation for <m>f</m></term>. It is straightforward to see that <m>T_f</m> is a non-negative, increasing function.
<idx><h>function of total variation</h></idx></p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-jordan-decomp-function">
<title>Jordan Decomposition Theorem</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>f</m> be a function of bounded variation on the interval <m>[a,b]</m>.
            Then there are increasing functions <m>h,g</m> on <m>[a,b]</m> such that <m>f = g -h</m>.        
            <idx><h>Jordan Decomposition Theorem</h><h>for functions</h></idx>
        </p>
    </statement>

    <proof>
<p>Take <m>a\leq c &lt; d \leq b</m>.
    Note that
<md> TV(f|_{[c,d]}) \geq |f(c) - f(d)|.</md>
Thus
<md>
\begin{aligned}
(T_f + f)(d) - (T_f + f)(c) &amp;=
        f(d) - f(c) + TV(f|_{[c,d]})\\
        &amp;\geq f(d) - f(c) + |f(d) - f(c)| \\
        &amp; \geq 0,
\end{aligned}
</md>
and
<md>
\begin{aligned}
(T_f - f)(d) - (T_f - f)(c) &amp;= 
        TV(f|_{[c,d]}) - f(d) + f(c) \\
        &amp;\geq |f(d) - f(c)| - f(d) + f(c) \\
        &amp;\geq 0.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Hence, the functions <m>T_f + f</m> and <m>T_f - f</m> are increasing functions.
    Let
<md> g = \frac{1}{2}(T_f + f) \text{ and } h = \frac{1}{2}(T_f - f).</md>
Then <m>f = g - h</m>, and so <m>f</m> is the difference of two increasing functions.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>

<definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>f</m> be a function of bounded variation.
            Let <m>g = \frac{1}{2}(T_f + f)</m> and <m>h = \frac{1}{2}(T_f - f)</m>.
            Then <m>f = g - h</m> is called the Jordan decomposition of <m>f</m>.
            <idx><h>Jordan decomposition</h><h>of a function</h></idx>
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>

<remark>
<p>Note that for a function of bounded variation
<md>
\begin{aligned}
TV(f) &amp;= T_f(b) = \frac{1}{2}(T_f + f)(b) +  \frac{1}{2}(T_f - f)(b) \\
&amp;= TV(\frac{1}{2}(T_f + f)) + TV(\frac{1}{2}(T_f - f)).
\end{aligned}
</md>
Thus, the total variation of <m>f</m> is given by the sum of the total variations of  the Jordan decomposition of <m>f</m>.</p>
<p>Recall that for a signed measure <m>\nu</m>, the total variation of <m>\nu</m> is given by <m>|\nu|(X) = \nu_+(X) + \nu_-(X)</m>, where <m>\nu = \nu_+ - \nu_-</m> is the Jordan decomposition of <m>\nu</m>.</p>
</remark>

<proposition xml:id="prop-total-var-cont">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>f</m> be a function of bounded variation on a closed interval <m>[a,b]</m>.
            If <m>f</m> is a right-continuous function then <m>T_f</m> is right-continuous.
            In particular, there are right-continuous increasing functions <m>g, h</m> such that <m>f = g- h</m>.        
        </p>
    </statement>

    <proof>
<p>Suppose that <m>f</m> is right-continuous. 
    Take <m>x_0 \in [a,b)</m>.
    Let <m>\alpha = \lim_{x \rightarrow x_0^+} T_f(x) - T_f(x_0)</m>.
    Since <m>T_f</m> is increasing, <m>\alpha \geq 0</m>.
    We want to show that <m>\alpha = 0</m>.</p>
<p>Fix <m>\varepsilon &gt; 0</m>.
    By the right-continuity of <m>f</m> and definition of <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow x_0^+}T_f(x)</m>, there exists <m>\delta &gt; 0</m> such that
<md>|f(x_0+h) - f(x_0)|&lt; \varepsilon</md>
and
<md>T_f(x_0+h) - \lim_{x\rightarrow x_0^+}T_f(x) &lt; \varepsilon</md>
when <m>0&lt;h&lt;\delta</m>.
Fix <m>h</m> with <m>0&lt;h&lt;\delta</m>.
    Choose a partition
<md>P = \{x_0 &lt; x_1 &lt; \ldots &lt; x_n = x_0 + h\}</md>
so that
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{k=1}^n|f(x_k) - f(x_{k-1})| &amp;\geq 
        \frac{3}{4}TV(f|_{[x_0,x_0+h]})\\
        &amp;= \frac{3}{4}(T_f(x_0+h) - T_f(x_0))\\
        &amp;\geq \frac{3}{4}(\lim_{x\rightarrow x_0^+}T_f(x) - T_f(x_0))\\
        &amp;\geq \frac{3}{4}\alpha.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Thus,
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{k=2}^n |f(x_k)-f(x_{k-1})| &amp;\geq 
        \frac{3}{4}\alpha - |f(x_1) - f(x_0)|\\
        &amp;&gt; \frac{3}{4}\alpha - \varepsilon.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Now take a partition of <m>[x_0,x_1]</m>,
<md> Q = \{x_0 =t_0 &lt; t_1 &lt; \ldots &lt; t_m=x_1\}</md>
such that
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{k=1}^m|f(x_k) - f(x_{k-1})| 
        \geq \frac{3}{4}TV(f|_{[x_0,x_1]}).
\end{aligned}
</md>
Then
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{k=1}^m|f(x_k) - f(x_{k-1})| 
        &amp;\geq \frac{3}{4}TV(f|_{[x_0,x_1]})\\
        &amp;= \frac{3}{4}(T_f(x_1) - T_f(x_0)) \\
        &amp;\geq \frac{3}{4}(\lim_{x\rightarrow x_0^+}T_f(x) - T_f(x_0)) \\
        &amp; = \frac{3}{4}\alpha.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Noting that <m>P \cup Q</m> is a partition of <m>[x_0,x_0+h]</m>, we have that
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\alpha + \varepsilon &amp;&gt; \left(\lim_{x\rightarrow x_0^+}T_f(x) - T_f(x_0)\right) + \left(T_f(x_0+h) - \lim_{x\rightarrow x_0^+}T_f(x)\right)\\
        &amp;= T_f(x_0 + h) - T_f(x_0) \\
        &amp;\geq \sum_{k=1}^m|f(t_k) - f(t_{k-1})| + \sum_{j=2}^n|f(x_j) - f(x_{j-1}| \\
        &amp;&gt; \frac{3}{4}\alpha + \frac{3}{4}\alpha - \varepsilon\\
        &amp;= \frac{3}{2}\alpha - \varepsilon.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Hence <m>\frac{\alpha}{2} &gt; 2 \varepsilon</m>, or <m>\alpha &lt; 4 \varepsilon</m>.
    As this holds for all <m>\varepsilon&gt;0</m>, it follows that <m>\alpha = 0</m> and so <m>T_f</m> is right-continuous.</p>
<p>The Jordan decomposition of <m>f</m>,
<md> f = \frac{1}{2}(T_f + f) - \frac{1}{2}(T_f - f)</md>
shows that <m>f</m> is the difference of two increasing, right-continuous functions.</p>
</proof>
</proposition>

<theorem xml:id="thm-signed-meas-1-1-bv">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>\mu</m> be a signed Borel measure on <m>[a,b]</m> be a signed Borel measure on <m>[a,b]</m>.
            Then <m>f(x) = \nu([a,x])</m> is a right-continuous function of bounded variation.</p>
            <p>Conversely, if <m>f</m> is a right-continuous function of bounded variation on <m>[a,b]</m>, then there is a unique Borel signed measure <m>\mu</m> on <m>[a,b]</m> such that <m>\mu([a,x]) = f(x)</m> for all <m>x \in [a,b]</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>

    <proof>
<p>The first part of the theorem is dealt with in <xref ref="lem-signed-meas-to-bv" />.</p>
<p>Let <m>f</m> be a right-continuous function  of bounded variation on <m>[a,b]</m>.
    Let <m>f = g - h</m> be the Jordan decomposition of <m>f</m>.
    By <xref ref="prop-total-var-cont" />, <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> are right-continuous.
    By <xref ref="cor-cdf-and-Borel" />, there are Borel measures <m>\nu_1</m> and <m>\nu_2</m> on <m>[a,b]</m> so that <m>g</m> is the cumulative distribution function for <m>\nu_1</m> and <m>h</m> is the cumulative distribution function for <m>\nu_2</m>.
    Let <m>\nu = \nu_1 - \nu_2</m>.
    Then <m>\nu</m> is a signed Borel measure on <m>[a,b]</m>, and for any <m>x \in [a,b]</m>
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\nu([a,x]) &amp;= \nu_1([a,x]) - \nu_2([a,x]) \\
        &amp;= g(x) - h(x) \\
        &amp;= f(x).
\end{aligned}
</md></p>
</proof>
</theorem>

<p><xref ref="thm-signed-meas-1-1-bv" /> gives a correspondence between right-continuous functions of bounded variation, and signed Borel measures on <m>[a,b]</m>. We have a Jordan decomposition for functions of bounded variation, and we have a Jordan decomposition for signed measures. We will now show that the correspondence given in <xref ref="thm-signed-meas-1-1-bv" /> sends the Jordan decomposition for functions to the Jordan decomposition for the signed measures, and vice versa.</p>

<proposition xml:id="prop-jordan-decomp-measure-and-function">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>f</m> be a right-continuous function of bounded variation on the closed interval <m>[a,b]</m>, and let <m>\nu</m> be the signed Borel measure satisfying <m>\nu([a,x]) = f(x)</m> from <xref ref="thm-signed-meas-1-1-bv" />.
    Let <m>f = f_1 - f_2</m> be the Jordan decomposition for <m>f</m> and <m>\nu = \nu_+ - \nu_-</m> be the Jordan decomposition for <m>\nu</m>.
    Then <m>f_1</m> is the cumulative distribution function for <m>\nu_+</m> and <m>f_2</m> is the cumulative distribution function for <m>\nu_-</m>.</p>
<p>Conversely, if <m>\nu</m> is a signed measure on <m>[a,b]</m> with Jordan decomposition <m>\nu = \nu_+- \nu_-</m>.
    If <m>f_1</m> is the cumulative distribution function for <m>\nu_+</m> and <m>f_2</m> is the cumulative distribution function for <m>\nu_-</m>, then, up to the addition of a constant to <m>f_1</m> and <m>f_2</m>, <m>f = f_1 -f_2</m> is the Jordan decomposition of the function <m>f(x) = \nu([a,x])</m>.</p>
<p>In both cases, we get that the total variation of <m>\nu</m> is equal to the total variation of <m>f</m>, i.e. <m>|\nu|([a,x]) = TV(f)</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
<p>Let <m>f</m> be a right-continuous function of bounded variation and let <m>\nu</m> be the signed Borel measure satisfying <m>\nu([a,x]) = f(x)</m> (which exists by <xref ref="thm-signed-meas-1-1-bv" />).
    Let <m>f = f_1 - f_2</m> be the Jordan decomposition of <m>f</m>, and <m>\nu = \nu_+ - \nu_-</m> be the Jordan decomposition of <m>\nu</m>.
    By <xref ref="cor-cdf-and-Borel" />, there are Borel measures <m>\nu_1</m> and <m>\nu_2</m> on <m>[a,b]</m> so that <m>\nu_1((c,d]) = f_1(d) -f_1(c)</m> and <m>\nu_2((c,d]) = f_2(d) -f_2(c)</m> for intervals <m>(c,d] \subseteq [a,b]</m>.</p>
<p>Note that <m>\nu = \nu_1 - \nu_2</m> and so, by <xref ref="prop-total-variation" />,
<md>
\begin{aligned}
|\nu|([a,b]) &amp;\leq \nu_1([a,b]) + \nu_2([a,b]) \\
        &amp;= f_1(b) - f_1(a) + f_2(b) - f_2(a) \\
        &amp;= TV(f).
\end{aligned}
</md>
Let <m>P</m> be a partition of <m>[a,b]</m>, by <xref ref="prop-bv-signed-meas-as-sup" />,
<md> V(f,P)\leq |\nu|([a,b]).</md>
Thus <m>TV(f) \leq |\nu([a,b])</m>.
    Hence <m>TV(f) = |\nu|([a,b])</m>.</p>
<p>Let <m>\nu</m> be a signed Borel measure on <m>[a,b]</m>, and define <m>f(x) = \nu([a,x])</m> for <m>x \in [a,b]</m>.
    Then <m>f</m> is right-continuous and of bounded variation by <xref ref="thm-signed-meas-1-1-bv" />,
    Let <m>\nu = \nu_+ - \nu_-</m> be the Jordan decomposition of <m>\nu</m>.
    Define functions <m>f^+1</m> and <m>f^-</m> on <m>[a,b]</m> by <m>f^+(x) = \nu_+([a,x])</m> and <m>f^-(x) = \nu_-([a,x])</m>.
    By <xref ref="thm-cdf" />, <m>f^+</m> and <m>f^-</m> are increasing, right continuous functions.
    Since <m>\nu = \nu_+ - \nu_-</m>, it follows that <m>f = f^+ - f^-</m>.
    Let <m>f_1 = \frac{1}{2}(T_f + f)</m> and <m>f_2 = \frac{1}{2}(T_f - f)</m> be the Jordan decomposition of <m>f</m>.
    By <xref ref="cor-cdf-and-Borel" />, there are Borel measures <m>\nu_1</m> and <m>\nu_2</m> whose cumulative distribution functions are <m>f_1</m> and <m>f_2</m>, respectively.</p>
<p>Note that <m>\nu = \nu_1 - \nu_2</m>.
    By <xref ref="prop-total-variation" /> for each <m>x \in [a,b]</m>
<md>
\begin{aligned}
|\nu|([a,x]) &amp;\leq \nu_1([a,x]) + \nu_2([a,x]) \\
        &amp;= (f_1(x) - f_1(a)) + (f_2(x) - f_2(a)) \\
        &amp;= \frac{1}{2}[T_f(x) + f(x) - T_f(a) - f(a) + T_f(x) - f(x) - T_f(a) + f(a)]\\
        &amp;= T_f(x).
\end{aligned}
</md>
By <xref ref="prop-bv-signed-meas-as-sup" />, <m>T_f(x) \leq |\nu|([a,x])</m> for all <m>x \in X</m>, and thus
<md>T_f(x) = |\nu|([a,x]).</md>
We have now,
<md>
\begin{aligned}
f(x) + T_f(x) &amp;= f^+(x) - f^-(x) + |\nu|([a,x]) \\
        &amp;= f^+(x) - f^-(x) + (f^+(x) - f^+(a)) + (f^-(x) - f^-(a))\\
        &amp;= 2f^+(x) - f^-(a) - f^+(a)
\end{aligned}
</md>
Hence
<md> f^+ = \frac{1}{2}(T_f + f) + C,</md>
where <m>C = \frac{1}{2}(f^+(a) + f^-(a))</m>.
    It follows that
<md> f^- = \frac{1}{2}(T_f -f) + C.</md>
</p>
</proof>
</proposition>

</section>